But, like, I know there were a few times where there'd be, like, fire traps

00:35:17.360 --> 00:35:20.840
or there'd be, like, a booby trap where, like, darts are coming out at you

00:35:20.840 --> 00:35:22.760
and stuff like that. Like, I guess that's dungeon-y.

00:35:23.420 --> 00:35:25.740
Yeah, there was also some points where, like,

00:35:25.880 --> 00:35:28.360
there would be an entrance to the north and the south.

00:35:29.100 --> 00:35:31.720
And regardless of whether you went north or south,

00:35:31.940 --> 00:35:36.260
you had the same enemy encounter on both sides.

00:35:36.740 --> 00:35:40.340
And, like, that just, like, am I in some kind of Mobius strip?

00:35:40.360 --> 00:35:42.460
Like, what is actually happening here?

00:35:42.460 --> 00:35:47.560
And so that, by virtue of at least being something notable,

00:35:47.980 --> 00:35:50.260
I guess, is something like that.

00:35:50.420 --> 00:35:54.720
There was also a point where I found a statue of what looked like a goddess.

00:35:54.840 --> 00:35:59.600
And that kind of called me back to the story in the manual

00:35:59.600 --> 00:36:02.360
where you, like, use the staff on it.

00:36:02.640 --> 00:36:09.420
And so I went in there and I took Moonglow or Moongirl's staff

00:36:09.420 --> 00:36:10.900
and used it on her.

00:36:11.120 --> 00:36:13.540
And then when I got back to the actual, like,

00:36:14.480 --> 00:36:16.260
screen of where things are happening,

00:36:16.560 --> 00:36:20.320
there was just no statue there anymore.

00:36:20.320 --> 00:36:22.880
So I don't know if I triggered something,

00:36:22.880 --> 00:36:26.660
if I got teleported somewhere, if I destroyed the statue.

00:36:26.900 --> 00:36:27.900
Again, no feedback.

00:36:27.900 --> 00:36:29.160
So who knows what I did.

00:36:29.380 --> 00:36:32.260
That might be the first interesting thing described, though,

00:36:32.360 --> 00:36:36.420
because the booby traps and the darts and the falling blocks,

00:36:36.540 --> 00:36:39.040
like, that all feels very passive stuff for me.

00:36:39.040 --> 00:36:40.420
Like, I guess my point is,

00:36:40.480 --> 00:36:45.400
is that Dungeons & Dragons isn't just a game of brute force warriors

00:36:45.400 --> 00:36:47.680
where you just, like, run into a room and it's like,

00:36:47.800 --> 00:36:49.460
the GM's like, ha, more skeletons.

00:36:49.480 --> 00:36:50.620
And you go fight them.

00:36:50.620 --> 00:36:51.860
Yeah, it's not Gauntlet. Right.

00:36:52.000 --> 00:36:52.860
You're supposed, yeah, exactly.

00:36:53.020 --> 00:36:55.380
You're supposed to, like, actually explore

00:36:55.380 --> 00:36:58.940
and find, like, hidden parts of the dungeon

00:36:58.940 --> 00:37:01.900
that unlock other additional content and stuff.

00:37:02.300 --> 00:37:04.940
All that to say that this game doesn't do that.

00:37:04.940 --> 00:37:06.980
And these eight warriors who could, you know,

00:37:06.980 --> 00:37:08.880
they are different classes and everything.

00:37:08.880 --> 00:37:10.320
They have different skill sets.

00:37:10.500 --> 00:37:13.040
They're basically just being used for combat.

00:37:13.060 --> 00:37:19.280
And so this whole game just becomes a fight your way through a dungeon

00:37:19.280 --> 00:37:21.720
until you find a dragon simulator,

00:37:21.720 --> 00:37:25.300
which is not a simulator I expected to play in 2026,

00:37:25.300 --> 00:37:26.200
but here we are.

00:37:26.200 --> 00:37:31.100
And I question that part of the goal

00:37:31.100 --> 00:37:35.320
because I understand they wanted to make an action game first and foremost.

00:37:35.500 --> 00:37:38.320
But even just, like, with these characters,

00:37:38.580 --> 00:37:39.620
whether you know them or not,

00:37:39.620 --> 00:37:42.040
I have no idea who any of these people are.

00:37:43.000 --> 00:37:44.320
But that aside,

00:37:44.660 --> 00:37:46.040
you would think that, like,

00:37:46.060 --> 00:37:49.020
they would each have more of their strong suits or whatever.

00:37:49.020 --> 00:37:52.540
But when you watch a complete playthrough of this game,

00:37:52.540 --> 00:37:54.520
which is only 23 minutes, by the way,

00:37:54.740 --> 00:37:56.420
for someone who knows what they're doing,

00:37:56.740 --> 00:38:00.200
they're not, like, constantly switching characters

00:38:00.200 --> 00:38:02.920
like I was doing to try to figure out what the hell to do.

00:38:03.100 --> 00:38:04.440
When you know what you're doing,

00:38:04.460 --> 00:38:07.160
you really are just one, maybe two characters

00:38:07.660 --> 00:38:08.720
the entire run,

00:38:08.720 --> 00:38:11.160
and everybody else is just happening through menus

00:38:11.160 --> 00:38:13.020
because you can cast spells that way.

00:38:13.180 --> 00:38:16.080
And so the game actually winds up becoming,

00:38:16.280 --> 00:38:19.240
and I get that, like, that's a perfect player or whatever,

00:38:19.240 --> 00:38:22.640
but it winds up becoming a game with very little of the mechanics

00:38:22.640 --> 00:38:25.260
that were actually laid down in the game

00:38:25.260 --> 00:38:27.800
and mostly just a how hard,

00:38:27.980 --> 00:38:29.860
how fast and how hard can you beat up enemies

00:38:29.860 --> 00:38:31.440
and can you do it, you know,

00:38:31.560 --> 00:38:32.600
better than they can do it to you.

00:38:33.600 --> 00:38:36.800
Yeah, it also kind of makes it feel like a puzzle with a solution,

00:38:37.060 --> 00:38:38.460
which I never really,

00:38:38.940 --> 00:38:41.920
you don't really feel good when an RPG feels like that.

00:38:43.160 --> 00:38:44.300
No, I don't know.

00:38:44.960 --> 00:38:46.120
A quick aside,

00:38:46.340 --> 00:38:48.080
but, Mike, you mentioned the YouTube,

00:38:48.520 --> 00:38:50.280
you know, speedruns and stuff.

00:38:50.460 --> 00:38:52.060
I just want to give a quick shout-out

00:38:52.060 --> 00:38:53.460
to, like, the absolute hero

00:38:53.460 --> 00:38:56.220
that learned to play this game inside and out

00:38:56.220 --> 00:38:57.300
so they could play it on YouTube

00:38:57.300 --> 00:38:59.520
because I know that that was probably, like,

00:38:59.560 --> 00:39:01.220
a very horrible experience.

00:39:01.940 --> 00:39:03.140
And, like, honestly,

00:39:03.300 --> 00:39:05.740
when I was, like, playing it,

00:39:05.780 --> 00:39:07.600
I, like, looked for this YouTube video

00:39:07.600 --> 00:39:10.900
and I was like, I cannot believe someone actually is,

00:39:11.060 --> 00:39:13.620
had played this enough to be good enough at this game

00:39:13.620 --> 00:39:15.340
to kind of give me a quick, like,

00:39:15.680 --> 00:39:18.900
25-minute, like, full experience of this

00:39:18.900 --> 00:39:22.240
and just be done with it because I feel like that sounds like a nightmare to me.

00:39:22.480 --> 00:39:24.380
If we're going mask off, Joe,

00:39:24.560 --> 00:39:25.640
I would just say that, like,

00:39:26.320 --> 00:39:28.160
after 15 minutes with this game,

00:39:28.200 --> 00:39:31.100
I think I put down the controller in defeat

00:39:31.100 --> 00:39:32.100
and just was kind of like,

00:39:32.160 --> 00:39:33.880
I don't want to do this anymore.

00:39:33.880 --> 00:39:38.500
I don't want to learn how to play this game in particular.

00:39:38.940 --> 00:39:40.580
There was something about it

00:39:40.580 --> 00:39:42.680
and I think it was what we were describing

00:39:42.680 --> 00:39:44.860
with the menus and just the points of friction and everything,

00:39:45.020 --> 00:39:48.780
but especially when you don't actually know

00:39:48.780 --> 00:39:50.680
how to play the game yet.

00:39:50.820 --> 00:39:52.480
Like, you don't know what could happen

00:39:53.860 --> 00:39:55.440
in the context of the game.

00:39:55.540 --> 00:39:56.940
Like, I always think about

00:39:56.940 --> 00:39:58.320
when I was young,

00:39:58.800 --> 00:40:00.140
first playing video games,

00:40:00.180 --> 00:40:02.200
you don't even understand necessarily

00:40:02.200 --> 00:40:05.360
that there is, like, a finite experience.

00:40:05.360 --> 00:40:08.440
You think, like, especially in 64 games for me

00:40:08.440 --> 00:40:11.220
when I was exploring, you think, like, things might change

00:40:11.220 --> 00:40:12.920
or different things might happen.

00:40:13.100 --> 00:40:14.880
Like, anything can happen in these worlds

00:40:14.880 --> 00:40:16.600
and then you eventually figure out, like,

00:40:16.660 --> 00:40:17.820
okay, no, this is all it is

00:40:17.820 --> 00:40:19.400
and then you master those skills or whatever

00:40:19.400 --> 00:40:21.260
and that takes time. That's why when you're a kid,

00:40:21.400 --> 00:40:23.100
you can spend, you know,

00:40:23.460 --> 00:40:25.500
two to three years playing Super Mario 64.

00:40:26.520 --> 00:40:28.140
I can't do that here

00:40:28.140 --> 00:40:29.840
with Advanced Dungeons & Dragons

00:40:29.840 --> 00:40:31.260
because thankfully,

00:40:31.260 --> 00:40:32.640
I was able to recognize early

00:40:32.640 --> 00:40:34.320
that it wasn't worth the time

00:40:34.320 --> 00:40:37.160
it was putting in. Like, it's not a good enough game

00:40:37.160 --> 00:40:39.520
to want to put in the time

00:40:39.520 --> 00:40:41.140
to learn its systems

00:40:41.140 --> 00:40:43.920
and I feel like they knew that.

00:40:43.920 --> 00:40:45.380
They had to know that.

00:40:45.460 --> 00:40:47.520
But then they laid it upon us anyway

00:40:48.780 --> 00:40:50.760
and so much is tarnished here.

00:40:50.960 --> 00:40:52.880
The Dungeons & Dragons name,

00:40:53.120 --> 00:40:55.180
the Dragonlance name,

00:40:55.280 --> 00:40:56.500
because I didn't even know what that was

00:40:56.500 --> 00:40:57.860
and now I have zero interest

00:40:57.860 --> 00:40:59.280
in, like, exploring that world

00:40:59.280 --> 00:41:00.360
because of this game

00:41:00.360 --> 00:41:03.800
and just the idea of, like,

00:41:04.340 --> 00:41:05.880
Dungeons & Dragons video games.

00:41:06.000 --> 00:41:07.660
It's like, I feel like

00:41:07.660 --> 00:41:11.240
my perception of Dungeons & Dragons

00:41:11.240 --> 00:41:12.380
and the title of video games

00:41:12.380 --> 00:41:14.180
means the game's not good

00:41:14.180 --> 00:41:16.880
and it might be because of this game

00:41:16.880 --> 00:41:18.600
because I owned this,

00:41:18.740 --> 00:41:19.940
I still own this game.

00:41:20.080 --> 00:41:21.700
This came with the NES

00:41:21.700 --> 00:41:23.320
that I bought off eBay, like,

00:41:23.720 --> 00:41:24.540
20 years ago.

00:41:24.740 --> 00:41:26.600
Wow. And it came with it

00:41:26.600 --> 00:41:29.480
and, yeah, what a, no wonder the guy was getting rid of it.

00:41:31.900 --> 00:41:33.240
He was like, you can have the NES

00:41:33.240 --> 00:41:34.440
but you must play

00:41:34.440 --> 00:41:35.700
Advanced Dungeons & Dragons

00:41:35.700 --> 00:41:36.520
Heroes of the Lands.

00:41:37.200 --> 00:41:38.180
And you've been trying

00:41:38.180 --> 00:41:39.400
to get rid of it ever since.

00:41:39.600 --> 00:41:41.360
Like, whoever has it has to try and get rid of it.

00:41:41.420 --> 00:41:43.920
It's a curse. Now, I don't know

00:41:43.920 --> 00:41:44.840
if that's true, though,

00:41:44.920 --> 00:41:46.620
about the other Dungeons & Dragons games.

00:41:46.780 --> 00:41:48.080
There's a ton more

00:41:48.080 --> 00:41:49.540
and there's a few more,

00:41:49.640 --> 00:41:50.500
I think, on the NES

00:41:50.500 --> 00:41:51.540
that we will play.

00:41:53.320 --> 00:41:55.980
But do you guys have any experience

00:41:55.980 --> 00:41:58.500
with Dungeons & Dragons video games

00:41:58.500 --> 00:41:59.940
outside of this NES entry?

00:42:00.280 --> 00:42:02.660
Does Baldur's Gate 3 count?

00:42:03.180 --> 00:42:04.060
It does.

00:42:04.400 --> 00:42:05.460
I don't know. Yes.

00:42:06.180 --> 00:42:07.640
But Baldur's Gate is just,

00:42:07.760 --> 00:42:09.240
instead of Dragonlance,

00:42:09.240 --> 00:42:11.080
it's for Forgotten Realms

00:42:11.080 --> 00:42:15.980
and between Baldur's Gate 1 through 3,

00:42:16.300 --> 00:42:18.220
Neverwinter Nights, Icewind Dale,

00:42:18.220 --> 00:42:20.820
like, these are all competent,

00:42:20.880 --> 00:42:22.800
if not great CRPGs

00:42:25.200 --> 00:42:27.480
that very much capture

00:42:27.480 --> 00:42:31.420
the idea of D&D,

00:42:31.420 --> 00:42:32.420
at least more so

00:42:32.420 --> 00:42:33.780
than anything on the NES.

00:42:35.080 --> 00:42:36.480
And some would say

00:42:36.480 --> 00:42:38.980
get pretty close to that idea

00:42:38.980 --> 00:42:42.100
of what D&D on a video game should be,

00:42:42.160 --> 00:42:43.040
especially with, like,

00:42:43.080 --> 00:42:44.560
the modding on Neverwinter Nights

00:42:44.560 --> 00:42:45.480
and yada, yada, yada.

00:42:45.480 --> 00:42:47.800
So, yeah, I have enough

00:42:47.800 --> 00:42:49.280
to compare to that, like,

00:42:49.340 --> 00:42:51.120
this is purely disappointment.

00:42:52.120 --> 00:42:53.920
Right. And I think that, like,

00:42:53.940 --> 00:42:55.400
I never really seek out

00:42:55.400 --> 00:42:57.240
a D&D-style game,

00:42:57.380 --> 00:42:58.240
although I did, you know,

00:42:58.300 --> 00:42:59.620
like I said, loved Baldur's Gate 3,

00:42:59.840 --> 00:43:02.360
but, like, the reason I never really seek it out

00:43:02.360 --> 00:43:04.080
is because I have a hard time imagining,

00:43:04.080 --> 00:43:05.240
it just seems like something

00:43:05.240 --> 00:43:07.260
that's so hard to translate

00:43:07.960 --> 00:43:09.540
to a video game.

00:43:09.580 --> 00:43:10.600
I mean, you're literally

00:43:10.600 --> 00:43:14.620
just purely have open imagination,

00:43:14.640 --> 00:43:16.220
like, where you can,

00:43:16.220 --> 00:43:19.000
you can do and say anything

00:43:19.000 --> 00:43:19.860
and there has to be

00:43:19.860 --> 00:43:20.780
another person there

00:43:20.780 --> 00:43:21.660
that can decide

00:43:21.660 --> 00:43:22.580
what will happen

00:43:22.580 --> 00:43:24.240
based on your actions.

00:43:24.280 --> 00:43:26.080
So it's, like, way more fluid

00:43:26.080 --> 00:43:27.220
than I can imagine

00:43:27.220 --> 00:43:28.960
video game code being.

00:43:28.960 --> 00:43:30.060
And maybe we're getting

00:43:30.060 --> 00:43:30.900
closer and closer

00:43:30.900 --> 00:43:31.820
to that point

00:43:31.820 --> 00:43:32.920
where that's not true anymore,

00:43:32.920 --> 00:43:34.100
but it's just hard.

00:43:34.260 --> 00:43:35.520
Even, like, I mean, you know,

00:43:35.520 --> 00:43:39.300
I know, like, I believe Elder Scrolls

00:43:39.300 --> 00:43:40.380
was kind of taking

00:43:40.380 --> 00:43:41.880
a lot of cues

00:43:41.880 --> 00:43:42.780
from Dungeons & Dragons.

00:43:43.020 --> 00:43:44.560
Was it even maybe originally supposed to be

00:43:44.560 --> 00:43:45.640
tied in somehow

00:43:45.640 --> 00:43:47.040
or something? I don't know,

00:43:47.140 --> 00:43:48.920
something like that. But it's,

00:43:49.060 --> 00:43:50.100
I think, like,

00:43:50.200 --> 00:43:51.360
the Elder Scrolls game would get as close

00:43:51.360 --> 00:43:53.320
as you can to being like, wow, you can be

00:43:53.320 --> 00:43:54.640
whatever you want in these games,

00:43:54.660 --> 00:43:55.920
but it's still not like,

00:43:56.440 --> 00:43:57.540
it'll never be like,

00:43:57.640 --> 00:43:58.460
oh, I'm sitting down,

00:43:58.880 --> 00:43:59.940
you know, with a DM

00:43:59.940 --> 00:44:00.840
and a group of friends

00:44:00.840 --> 00:44:02.820
and, like, we can do anything.

00:44:03.040 --> 00:44:03.880
You know what I mean?

00:44:03.940 --> 00:44:05.020
Like, anything we can think of

00:44:05.020 --> 00:44:07.120
can be done because the game

00:44:07.120 --> 00:44:08.480
is played on,

00:44:08.660 --> 00:44:09.880
the console is,

00:44:09.980 --> 00:44:12.760
like, your brain as opposed to, like,

00:44:13.180 --> 00:44:14.560
there has to be some sort of limitation

00:44:14.560 --> 00:44:15.680
in any video game. So it's like,

00:44:15.740 --> 00:44:17.320
I don't seek out

00:44:17.320 --> 00:44:18.500
games that are trying

00:44:18.500 --> 00:44:19.440
to do that because it seems like

00:44:19.440 --> 00:44:20.900
that's beyond a video game.

00:44:21.820 --> 00:44:23.380
Yeah, I think in a,

00:44:24.220 --> 00:44:25.980
with a good DM

00:44:25.980 --> 00:44:27.820
that isn't just trying

00:44:27.820 --> 00:44:30.360
to create a,

00:44:30.920 --> 00:44:32.360
like, improv space

00:44:32.360 --> 00:44:34.960
for people, I think that

00:44:34.960 --> 00:44:36.540
what ends up happening

00:44:36.540 --> 00:44:38.880
in a D&D session

00:44:38.880 --> 00:44:40.740
ends up getting to,

00:44:40.740 --> 00:44:41.700
like,

00:44:41.900 --> 00:44:43.000
the choices

00:44:43.000 --> 00:44:45.900
that a D&D game

00:44:45.900 --> 00:44:46.920
of the scope

00:44:46.920 --> 00:44:49.080
and polish

00:44:49.080 --> 00:44:50.340
of Baldur's Gate 3

00:44:50.340 --> 00:44:51.620
would give you.

00:44:51.760 --> 00:44:53.380
Like, there's still a lot of choice

00:44:53.380 --> 00:44:55.180
and consequence in a game like that.

00:44:55.320 --> 00:44:56.440
Yes, you can't say

00:44:56.440 --> 00:44:57.480
literally anything,

00:44:57.480 --> 00:44:59.080
but there'd probably

00:44:59.080 --> 00:45:00.760
be enough, like,

00:45:01.320 --> 00:45:02.800
shepherding to get you

00:45:02.800 --> 00:45:05.860
to a point where the end states

00:45:05.860 --> 00:45:07.800
of a Baldur's Gate quest

00:45:07.800 --> 00:45:08.860
would occur.

00:45:09.520 --> 00:45:11.720
Sure, yeah. So I agree

00:45:11.720 --> 00:45:14.340
that it's never going to feel as dynamic

00:45:14.340 --> 00:45:15.980
and real,

00:45:16.570 --> 00:45:19.400
but I think that

00:45:19.400 --> 00:45:20.220
that's still

00:45:20.220 --> 00:45:21.760
pretty damn close.

00:45:22.240 --> 00:45:24.300
Right. And I'm recognizing

00:45:24.300 --> 00:45:25.720
that, yeah, like, in a D&D session

00:45:25.720 --> 00:45:26.520
there's usually probably

00:45:26.520 --> 00:45:27.640
all kinds of, like,

00:45:27.740 --> 00:45:30.100
internal cheating happening by the DM

00:45:30.100 --> 00:45:31.000
where it's like, well, this is where

00:45:31.000 --> 00:45:32.520
the story's gonna go

00:45:32.520 --> 00:45:33.960
eventually. Or, you know,

00:45:34.020 --> 00:45:35.060
or in some way.

00:45:35.220 --> 00:45:36.040
But it's still, like,

00:45:36.120 --> 00:45:37.320
yeah, the dynamics

00:45:38.620 --> 00:45:39.720
of your decisions

00:45:39.720 --> 00:45:41.160
is more what I mean

00:45:41.160 --> 00:45:43.260
where it's like, it's,

00:45:44.080 --> 00:45:45.140
if it's done well,

00:45:45.220 --> 00:45:46.600
you can't really see behind the curtain.

00:45:46.740 --> 00:45:47.940
It should feel like,

00:45:48.300 --> 00:45:49.940
oh, you're actually just making any choice

00:45:49.940 --> 00:45:51.380
you want. Mm-hmm.

00:45:51.700 --> 00:45:53.960
And I'm sure in the current landscape

00:45:53.960 --> 00:45:55.240
somebody's gotta be

00:45:55.240 --> 00:45:57.460
looking into how do we capitalize

00:45:57.460 --> 00:45:58.320
on AI.

